{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792313344,"nonce":"DvGAHAR+y+wva3bWTZxPZbtHTAo=","title":"The web backend's first temporary handle is the canvas element's, so a canvas is never resized","message":"In a page, `canvas.width = …` and `canvas.height = …` failed on every call with \"Cannot set properties of undefined\", so the drawing buffer never took the size of the window, whatever the page did to the canvas: no resize, no pixel ratio.\n\n**Cause**\n\nThe agent holds three handles from the start: `navigator.gpu` (1), the canvas's WebGPU context (2) and the canvas element (3). The plugin hands out temporary handles, for objects it makes and releases in one batch, from a counter that began at 2, so its first temporary handle was 3. The first thing to use one is reading the device's features: the features list was stored under 3, replacing the canvas element, and released after the read, which deleted it. Every later `OffscreenCanvas.width =` found nothing under 3.\n\nThe counter also wrapped back to 3 and handed it out again once it reached its limit.\n\n**Fix**\n\nThe temporary handles begin after the three roots, and wrap to the first handle after them.\n\n**Seen in**\n\nashui's banner scene in a page. The failures were in the console from the first frame, and the canvas stayed the size the page first gave it.","files":null}]}